373				{Key: "esc (Path, running)", Description: "Cancel the trace and keep the hops already seen (Path tab)"},
374				{Key: "esc (Path, done)", Description: "Edit the target again, keeping its value (Path tab)"},
375				{Key: "enter (Trace Path button)", Description: "Trace the path to the form's target in a sub-level of the Diagnostics tab, Network › Path › target; Port and DNS Server are ignored by the trace (an info line says so); greyed out on a platform that cannot trace, with the reason in the footer (Diagnostics tab)"},
376				{Key: "esc (trace)", Description: "Cancel a running trace; once it ended, go back to the form with Target, Port and DNS Server kept (Diagnostics tab)"},
377				{Key: "↑ / ↓ / pgup / pgdown / home / end (trace)", Description: "Move in the hop table; every other key is ignored there except tab, which switches tab (Diagnostics tab)"},
378				{Key: "enter / ctrl+r / ctrl+o (trace)", Description: "Do nothing in the trace sub-level: to trace again, go back to the form with esc and press the Trace Path button; the trace always starts from this machine (Diagnostics tab)"},
379				{Key: "ctrl+o (Path)", Description: "Choose where the trace starts: this machine or a running container; ↑↓ to move, enter to choose, esc to close (Path tab)"},
380			},
381			Sections: []help.Section{
382				{
383					Title: "Diagnostics Tab — What it asks",
384					Body: "One target, one run. The checks follow from the target and from what has " +
385						"already failed, so there is nothing to select.\n\n" +
386						"DNS resolution     - the name, against the resolver your own traffic uses\n" +
387						"Reverse DNS        - the name an address publishes (literal targets only)\n" +
388						"Local route        - which interface carries this target, and from which address\n" +
389						"ICMP echo          - reachability; a filtered echo is a warning, never a failure\n" +
390						"TCP connect        - the port you named, and the answer that decides reachability\n" +
391						"TLS handshake      - whether the port speaks TLS at all\n" +
392						"Certificate chain  - verified against this machine's store, intermediates included\n" +
393						"Hostname match     - whether the certificate names the host you dialled\n" +
394						"Certificate expiry - the validity window, in days\n" +
395						"TLS version        - the negotiated protocol version\n" +
396						"HTTP response      - whether the service answers, over the scheme actually spoken",
397				},
398				{
399					Title: "Diagnostics Tab — Reading the verdicts",
400					Body: "OK      - the objective is met\n" +
401						"WARN    - met, but something is off: a certificate near expiry, a deprecated\n" +
402						"          version, a chain that verifies here and will not elsewhere\n" +
403						"FAIL    - the objective is not met\n" +
404						"N/A     - no meaning for this target, or blocked by a check above it\n" +
405						"UNKNOWN - the check could not look, which is not the same as a pass\n\n" +
406						"A stage whose dependency failed does not run: its checks come back N/A naming " +
407						"what blocked them, so a broken run reads as one failure and a named point of " +
408						"rupture rather than as ten red rows.\n\n" +
409						"Press enter on any row for what it means and what to do about it.",
410				},
411				indicator.Legend(),
412				{
413					Title: "Ports Tab — How it works",
414					Body: "Reads this machine's TCP and UDP sockets every 2 seconds, in this process — no " +
415						"container, no Docker. Shows the owning process name and PID where the system will " +
416						"name one. K terminates that process, after a confirmation, with the rights DevDesk " +
417						"itself has: another user's process or a service comes back refused rather than " +
418						"silently killed elsewhere.\n\n" +
419						"n toggles reverse DNS on the addresses. Host names only — port numbers stay " +
420						"numeric, because a service name would be DevDesk's guess and not the system's.\n\n" +
421						"While K is killing a process, the dot on its sockets (" + indicator.Glyph(indicator.Running, true) +
422						") is lit and the footer names the kill.",
423				},
424				{
425					Title: "Forward Tab — How it works",
426					Body: "Redirects traffic to a host:port, inside this process — no privilege, " +
427						"no container, nothing written to a system file. Use it to reach a port nothing " +
428						"published: a container's EXPOSEd port, or a service on another machine.\n\n" +
429						"N opens a form with a Type field, changed with ← →. TCP redirects a local port " +
430						"to a host:port. HTTP gives the target a name instead of a port: fill in Name " +
431						"and Target, and http://<name>:<proxy port> reaches it.\n\n" +
432						"A name must end in .localhost, such as api.localhost — nothing else is " +
433						"accepted. That suffix is reserved for this machine, so it needs no DNS entry and " +
434						"no privilege; any other name would not resolve. Every named route is served on " +
435						"the same port, network.proxy_port (8080 by default, in the network tab of the " +
436						"configuration), and the request's Host header picks the target — so " +
437						"http://api.localhost:8080 and http://app.localhost:8080 are two services on one " +
438						"port. The port stays in the URL because 80 is privileged. It is HTTP only: " +
439						"https would need a certificate your browser trusts. A name that is not known " +
440						"gets a page listing the ones that are.\n\n" +
441						"Everything listens on 127.0.0.1 only, so it is available to this machine and " +
442						"not to the network — a service kept off the LAN stays off it. A TCP forward's " +
443						"local port has to be 1024 or above, and cannot be the proxy's: anything below " +
444						"needs privileges DevDesk does not ask for, and there is no unprivileged way " +
445						"around that on Linux or macOS.\n\n" +
446						"The target is dialled once before the port is bound, so an address that answers " +
447						"nothing is refused immediately instead of failing later at the first client. A " +
448						"container's own address is reachable from the host on native Linux; under " +
449						"Docker Desktop it lives in the virtual machine and does not route out of it.\n\n" +
450						"Forwards are saved in ~/.devdesk/forwards.yaml and reopened when DevDesk " +
451						"starts. They survive a context switch — a local port pointed at a host:port " +
452						"belongs to no context — but the listener itself cannot outlive the process, so " +
453						"what comes back is the list, bound again.\n\n" +
454						"A forward that cannot be bound at launch — its port is taken, or its target is " +
455						"not up yet — is not deleted. Its row reads unbound, the last error says why, and " +
456						"space tries again. Space also pauses a live forward: the port is released and the " +
457						"row stays, paused, until you resume it. K deletes a forward for good, and asks " +
458						"first.",
459				},
460				{
461					Title: "Path Tab — How it works",
462					Body: "Traces the hops between this machine and a target, in this process — no " +
463						"container, no traceroute binary. Native Linux and WSL2 only: on macOS, Windows and WSL1 the tab says so " +
464						"and enter and ctrl+o are greyed out. IPv4 only: a host name is resolved to its IPv4 address. " +
465						"The column starts on this machine: the interface the target's traffic leaves by, " +
466						"with its source address, and the gateway, read from this machine's routing table, " +
467						"then the hops. Under WSL2 the source row names the WSL2 VM, and in NAT mode the " +
468						"first hop through the virtual switch is labelled Windows host.\n\n" +
469						"Type a host name or an IPv4 address, and press enter; a port is refused because " +
470						"the trace probes a host, not a service. Each hop is a UDP datagram sent to UDP " +
471						"port 33435 with a growing time-to-live; the router that drops it answers, and the " +
472						"table fills in as the answers come. No connection is made. Nothing is sent before you " +
473						"press enter.\n\n" +
474						"A silent hop is not a failure: many routers do not answer these probes, so the row " +
475						"reads silent and the trace goes on. The header says how a trace ended.\n\n" +
476						"When the target never answers, the silent hops at the end fold into one row, " +
477						"no response beyond hop N, and the last hop that answered reads last to answer. " +
478						"When this machine has no route to the target, the interface row reads no route " +
479						"and nothing is sent; undetermined means the routing table could not be read, " +
480						"which says nothing either way. DevDesk shows only what came back: it does not " +
481						"say why the rest stayed silent.\n\n" +
482						"ctrl+o opens Trace from: this machine, or a running container (Docker Engine on " +
483						"Linux only). The column then starts with the container, its network and the " +
484						"gateway it uses, and a row says the probes still leave from this machine: " +
485						"nothing runs inside the container.\n\n" +
486						"Docker Desktop and podman are not offered. With no running container, only this " +
487						"machine is offered. Reading the engine (ps, info, inspect) starts nothing. An " +
488						"engine reached through DOCKER_HOST or a remote context is not detected as remote: " +
489						"it can show another machine's network, and the trace still leaves from this machine.\n\n" +
490						"The bridge row appears only when this machine has the interface of the container's " +
491						"network bridge, read on this machine; otherwise no bridge is shown. A remote engine " +
492						"whose network is also called docker0 can match this machine's interface by name.\n\n" +
493						"When the container's bridge is not on this machine, the engine row reads " +
494						"internal path not visible (location unknown): the engine may run in a VM, remote " +
495						"or rootless, and DevDesk does not guess. When it cannot check, the row reads " +
496						"undetermined (bridge not verified). The hops are traced from this machine (the " +
497						"host, or the WSL VM when DevDesk runs under WSL); DevDesk cannot see inside the " +
498						"engine's VM.",
499				},
500				{
501					Title: "Interfaces Tab — How it works",
502					Body: "Lists this machine's network interfaces, read in this process — no container, " +
503						"no Docker. Name, state, MTU, hardware address, error counters and every address " +
504						"the interface carries. ctrl+r re-reads them.\n\n" +
505						"A dash is not a zero. RX err and TX err show a dash when the counters could not " +
506						"be read at all, which is a different thing from an interface that has dropped " +
507						"nothing; MTU shows one where the platform reports no figure, as Windows does " +
508						"for its loopback.\n\n" +
509						"This was the Topology tab. Its routing table, ARP cache and firewall summary " +
510						"were read by running ip and iptables in a container on the Docker Desktop VM, " +
511						"so they described the VM and not this machine. Nothing portable produces them, " +
512						"so they were removed rather than translated — and the route question moved to " +
513						"the Diagnostics tab, where it is asked about a target: the Local route check " +
514						"names the interface your traffic to that host actually leaves by.",
515				},
516				{
517					Title: "Where the checks run",
518					Body: "Everything here runs in this process, on this machine's network stack: the " +
519						"diagnostic checks, the socket table, the interfaces and the path trace alike. They answer for " +
520						"the resolver, the routing table, the sockets and the VPN you are actually on, " +
521						"and nothing needs Docker.\n\n" +
522						"The route trace used to be the exception. It ran traceroute in a container " +
523						"started with --network host, which on Docker Desktop is the Linux VM's " +
524						"namespace — so it traced the path from the VM and not from here, and the two " +
525						"are not the same network. It was removed rather than kept with a caveat.\n\n" +
526						"The trace is back, in this process: the Path tab sends its own probes from this " +
527						"machine's network stack, so it answers for the network you are actually on. The " +
528						"Local route check still names the interface and source address your traffic to a " +
529						"target leaves by — the split-tunnel answer, read from this machine.",
530				},
531			},
532		}
533	}
534

